  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Establishing the Foundations of a Belgian-Tunisian Learning Mobility Scheme</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Tunisian labour market is currently facing major challenges. According to data from the Tunisian Bureau of Statistics, by 2023 more than 15 percent of the population was unemployed. Unemployment particularly affects young people, as more than 39 percent of young people (15-24 years old) are unemployed. Tunisian youth face several challenges in the labour market. Their degrees and qualifications are not always adapted to the needs of business, there is a high number of informal jobs and increased competition. At the same time, Belgium is facing structural labour shortages in all economic sectors, according to a prospective labour market analysis for the next decade on the impact of digitalization on the Belgian labour market, published by the Belgian Federation of the Technology Sector, Agoria, in 2023.A circular mobility program for Tunisian students/graduates thereby offers a win-win solution. The overall objective of this project is for IOM to facilitate the continuation of such a learning mobility scheme (LMS) between Tunisia and Belgium, with a focus on the ICT sector (but not exclusively). The learning mobility scheme will offer graduating youngsters an international job experience (internship) or access to a traineeship with the aim of increasing their knowledge and skills and consequently increasing their employability once they return to Tunisia. This project therefore aims to develop a sustainable model for an learning mobility scheme that meets the needs of the Belgian labour market while at the same time contributing to the development of the Tunisian economy through a transfer of knowledge and skills.</narrative>
  </description>
